chromium-embedded-framework for Debian
======================================

This package has a NON-STANDARD build workflow that requires manual preparation
and uses BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES for technical reasons documented below.

CRITICAL: Chromium Source Requirement
--------------------------------------

CEF (Chromium Embedded Framework) requires Chromium browser source code as a
build input. Unlike typical Debian packages, this source is NOT included in the
package's orig.tar.gz and is NOT automatically downloaded during build.

MANDATORY BUILD PREPARATION
----------------------------

Before building this package, you MUST manually download the Debian chromium
source tarball and place it in the parent directory:

    # From the parent directory of chromium-embedded-framework/
    $ apt-get source --download-only chromium

This creates:
    chromium_142.0.7444.59.orig.tar.xz  (~961 MB)

The build system (debian/rules) will reference this file at:
    ../chromium_142.0.7444.59.orig.tar.xz

Build Workflow
--------------

1. Prepare build environment:

    $ cd /path/to/workspace/
    $ apt-get source --download-only chromium
    $ ls -lh chromium_142.0.7444.59.orig.tar.xz
    # Verify file exists and is ~961 MB

2. Extract/clone CEF package:

    # If using git:
    $ git clone <salsa-repo-url> chromium-embedded-framework

    # If using source package:
    $ dpkg-source -x chromium-embedded-framework_*.dsc

3. Build package:

    $ cd chromium-embedded-framework/
    $ dpkg-buildpackage -us -uc

The build process will:
- Step 1: Clone CEF repository (branch 7444) using automate-git.py
- Step 2: Extract chromium tarball to chromium_src/chromium/
- Step 3: Apply Debian patches to CEF (using quilt)
- Step 4: Build CEF binary distribution

Expected build time: 2-3 hours on modern hardware
Required disk space: ~40 GB total (Chromium ~6GB extracted + CEF build ~34GB)

================================================================================
BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES JUSTIFICATION
================================================================================

This package bundles several components that deviate from standard Debian
practice. Each bundled dependency is documented below with technical
justification.

1. LIBC++ (C++ Standard Library)
---------------------------------

Status: BUNDLED from Chromium's third_party/libc++
Reason: ABI INCOMPATIBILITY with system libc++

TECHNICAL DETAILS:

Chromium/CEF uses a custom libc++ build with Chromium-specific ABI settings:
  - ABI Namespace: std::__Cr:: (Chromium-specific)
  - ABI Version: 2
  - Custom configuration flags (ASAN instrumentation, hardening mode, etc.)

Debian's system libc++ (libc++-dev package) uses:
  - ABI Namespace: std::__1:: (standard LLVM)
  - ABI Version: 1
  - Different feature set (wide characters, localization)

ATTEMPTED SOLUTIONS (All Failed):

During package development, we extensively tested system libc++ alternatives:

  Build 108: System libc++ only
    Result: FAILED at task 78/77621
    Error: "implicit instantiation of undefined template 'std::basic_ostream'"
    Cause: ABI namespace mismatch (looking for std::__Cr::, found std::__1::)

  Build 109: Chromium's perfetto __config_site
    Result: FAILED at task 56/77627 (REGRESSION from Build 108)
    Error: "_LIBCPP_INSTRUMENTED_WITH_ASAN" empty macro breaks preprocessor
    Cause: Perfetto config has problematic empty macro definitions

  Build 110: HYBRID - system + namespace override (__1 → __Cr)
    Result: Reached task 102/77590 (BEST), but FAILED
    Error: "undefined reference to std::__Cr::setw/setfill"
    Cause: ABI version mismatch (symbols mangled with v2, config has v1)

  Build 111: HYBRID - system + namespace + version overrides
    Result: FAILED at task 71/77643 (REGRESSION from Build 110)
    Cause: Additional overrides created more symbol conflicts

  Build 113: Attempted Build 110 replica
    Result: FAILED at task 76/77683 with DIFFERENT errors
    Error: "no type named 'wstring' in namespace 'std'"
    Cause: System libc++ headers provide only forward declarations,
           not complete implementations required by Chromium

CONCLUSION:

System libc++ with manual overrides requires 4+ sed commands and still fails
due to incomplete header implementations. The wstring paradox (Build 113 error
despite _LIBCPP_HAS_NO_WIDE_CHARACTERS being undefined) proves system headers
are fundamentally incompatible with Chromium's expectations.

PRECEDENT:

Debian's src:chromium package ALSO uses bundled libc++ for identical ABI
compatibility reasons. This is not a CEF-specific issue, but a fundamental
Chromium build requirement.

IMPLEMENTATION:

debian/rules copies libc++ configuration from Chromium's perfetto submodule:
  chromium/src/third_party/perfetto/buildtools/libcxx_config/

Files bundled:
  - __config_site (ABI configuration)
  - __assertion_handler (custom assertion behavior)

These are placed at:
  chromium/src/buildtools/third_party/libc++/

Header files remain in Chromium tarball at:
  chromium/src/third_party/libc++/src/include/

LINTIAN OVERRIDE:

This will trigger: embedded-library usr/lib/libcef.so: libc++

Override justified because:
  - Chromium-specific ABI incompatible with system libc++
  - Upstream requirement, not packaging choice
  - Same approach as Debian's chromium package
  - Extensively tested alternatives (Builds 108-113)

2. DEPOT_TOOLS (Build Automation Tools)
----------------------------------------

Status: BUNDLED at chromium_embedded_framework/depot_tools/
Reason: NETWORK ISOLATION and BUILD REPRODUCIBILITY

TECHNICAL DETAILS:

depot_tools is Google's build automation toolkit (git, gclient, ninja, etc.).
CEF's automate-git.py expects depot_tools in PATH.

ATTEMPTED SOLUTIONS:

  Approach 1: Download depot_tools during build
    Result: VIOLATES Debian Policy
    Reason: Builds must be reproducible without network access

  Approach 2: System packages (git, python3, etc.)
    Result: FAILED - missing gclient, custom git wrappers
    Reason: depot_tools is not a standard system package

  Approach 3: Bundle depot_tools
    Result: SUCCESS
    Benefits:
      - Offline builds (network isolated)
      - Version pinned (reproducible)
      - Matches upstream CEF requirements

IMPLEMENTATION:

Build-time bundling:
  1. automate-git.py clones depot_tools if not present
  2. debian/rules PRESERVES depot_tools/ across clean
  3. Only chromium_src/ is removed during clean

This is Phase 1 approach - see debian/TODO-DEPOT-TOOLS.txt for:
  - Future Phase 2 (dedicated depot-tools package)
  - Phase 3 (integration with Debian tooling)

DEBIAN POLICY COMPLIANCE:

depot_tools is treated as a BUILD TOOL (like autoconf), not a library.
It is NOT installed to the system, only used during package build.

Files in binary package come from CEF/Chromium compilation, NOT depot_tools.

3. ROLLUP (JavaScript Bundler)
-------------------------------

Status: USES SYSTEM PACKAGE (rollup)
Reason: EXAMPLE OF SYSTEM LIBRARY USAGE

This is included as a POSITIVE example - we DO use system libraries when viable.

Chromium expects @rollup/wasm-node (contains pre-compiled WASM binary, stripped
from Debian tarball per policy). We successfully use system rollup via symlink:

  debian/rules creates:
    chromium/src/third_party/devtools-frontend/src/node_modules/@rollup/wasm-node/dist/bin/rollup
    → symlink to /usr/share/nodejs/rollup/dist/bin/rollup

  debian/control:
    Build-Depends: rollup

Result: BUILD.gn files find system rollup at expected path, no patches needed.

================================================================================
WHY THIS NON-STANDARD APPROACH?
================================================================================

Debian Bug #893448 (filed 2018, still open) requests a chromium-source binary
package to solve the chromium tarball problem. Until that exists, manual setup
is required.

Alternative approaches were considered and rejected:

❌ Embed chromium in CEF source package:
   - Would create ~1GB source package (unacceptable for Debian)
   - Violates Debian Policy: "do not put code where canonical version is elsewhere"
   - Duplicates Debian's chromium security patches

❌ Download chromium during build:
   - Violates Debian Policy: builds must be reproducible without network
   - Source packages must be buildable offline

✅ Current approach (manual chromium tarball):
   - Uses Debian's official chromium source (includes security patches)
   - Complies with Debian Policy (no embedded code, no network builds)
   - Documented workflow for maintainers/builders
   - Migration path when Bug #893448 is resolved

================================================================================
MODIFYING AND PATCHING
================================================================================

CEF-Specific Patches
--------------------

Patches applied to CEF source (not Chromium):

  Location: debian/patches/
  Format: quilt patch series
  Applied: During Step 3 of build (after CEF checkout, before compilation)

To modify CEF patches:

  $ cd chromium-embedded-framework/
  $ dpkg-buildpackage --build=source  # Extract source only
  $ cd chromium_src/cef/
  $ export QUILT_PATCHES=../../debian/patches
  $ quilt push -a    # Apply all patches
  $ quilt new my-fix.patch
  $ quilt add path/to/file
  $ vi path/to/file  # Make changes
  $ quilt refresh
  $ quilt header -e  # Add patch description
  $ cd ../..
  $ dpkg-source --commit

Chromium Build Configuration
-----------------------------

Chromium-specific build settings (not patches to code):

  Location: debian/rules lines 190-270
  Purpose: Configure libc++, rollup, BUILD.gn stubs

Key configuration sections:

  Lines 197-206: libc++ BUILD.gn stub creation
  Lines 229-244: libc++ __config_site setup (BUNDLED - see justification above)
  Lines 250-261: Rollup symlink (SYSTEM - see example above)

To modify libc++ configuration:

  WARNING: See "BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES JUSTIFICATION" section above.
  System libc++ has been extensively tested and FAILS.
  Only modify if you have new information about libc++ compatibility.

  If modifying anyway:
    1. Edit debian/rules lines 229-244
    2. Document reasoning in debian/changelog
    3. Reference this README.source section
    4. Test build thoroughly (expect failures)

To add new system library substitutions:

  PREFERRED: Follow rollup example (lines 250-261)
    - Create symlink at path BUILD.gn expects
    - Add Build-Depends in debian/control
    - Document in this README.source

  AVOID: Patching BUILD.gn files
    - Requires maintaining patches across upstream updates
    - Fragile to Chromium version changes

Version Compatibility
---------------------

CEF branch 7444 requires Chromium 142.0.7444.x sources.

The build system includes version compatibility fixes:
- CEF expects 142.0.7444.53 (upstream Chromium tag)
- Debian has 142.0.7444.59 (with Debian patches)
- debian/rules patches CHROMIUM_BUILD_COMPATIBILITY.txt during build

When updating to new CEF/Chromium versions:
1. Update CEF_BRANCH in debian/rules
2. Update chromium version in debian/rules --chromium-archive path
3. Update SHA256 hash in debian/rules --chromium-archive-sha256
4. Re-test bundled dependency assumptions (libc++ ABI may change)

================================================================================
ADVANCED BUILD TOPICS
================================================================================

GBP (git-buildpackage) Workflow
--------------------------------

This package is PARTIALLY compatible with gbp:

    $ gbp buildpackage --git-prebuild="test -f ../chromium_*.orig.tar.xz"

The .gbp.conf file includes a prebuild check to verify chromium tarball exists.

However, standard "gbp import-orig" workflow is NOT supported because:
- CEF orig.tar.gz contains only CEF source (~50MB)
- Chromium tarball is a build input, NOT part of the source package
- No mechanism exists to auto-download build-time source dependencies

pbuilder/sbuild Compatibility
------------------------------

NOT YET SUPPORTED.

These tools require special setup to make the chromium tarball available inside
the build chroot. Possible approaches:

1. Copy tarball into chroot before build:
    $ sudo cp chromium_*.orig.tar.xz /var/cache/pbuilder/build/*/tmp/buildd/*/..

2. Use bind mount to make tarball accessible

3. Wait for Debian Bug #893448 to be resolved (chromium-source package)

Debugging Build Issues
----------------------

Build log locations:
- automate-git.py output: shown in dpkg-buildpackage output
- CEF patch application: shown during Step 3 of build
- Chromium compilation: shown during Step 4 of build

Common issues:

1. "chromium tarball not found":
   → Run apt-get source --download-only chromium in parent directory

2. "Patch application failed":
   → Check debian/patches/series is correct
   → Verify patches apply: cd chromium_src/cef && quilt push -a

3. "Version mismatch":
   → Update CHROMIUM_BUILD_COMPATIBILITY.txt patch in debian/rules

4. "Disk space full":
   → Build requires ~40GB free space

5. "libc++ compilation errors" (std::__1:: vs std::__Cr::):
   → DO NOT attempt to use system libc++ (see BUNDLED DEPENDENCIES section)
   → Verify perfetto __config_site is being used (check build log)

6. "depot_tools lockfile errors":
   → Development issue only (not in clean builds)
   → Run: rm -f depot_tools/external_bin/gsutil/*.locked

================================================================================
FUTURE MIGRATION
================================================================================

When Debian Bug #893448 is resolved and chromium-source package exists:

debian/control will change to:
    Build-Depends: chromium-source (>= 142.0)

debian/rules will change to:
    --chromium-archive=/usr/src/chromium/chromium.tar.xz

The manual chromium tarball setup documented in this file will no longer be
needed.

NOTE: Bundled libc++ will STILL be required even with chromium-source package,
due to fundamental ABI incompatibility with system libc++.

